  "body": "This is a change for stream creators only, and not for stream consumers. It is motivated by fixing #135.\n\nNow that we have benchmarks, we can try things like this.\n\nThis fixes #135 since the calls to [[onWrite]] are no longer recursive, but instead put onto the microtask queue.\n\nThere is a semantic change: if the underlying sink is able to acknowledge writes synchronously, that will _not_ help the stream transition back to writable.\n\nBenchmarks show no real difference (i.e., the variation between two runs of the async version is similar to the variation between the async and sync versions). Raw results, with filenames replaced with descriptive names (I ran both sync and async benchmarks twice):\n\n```\nasync #2 (current) vs. sync #1 (current - 3)\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n16,1024,15,0,sync,0.3,0,0,0,sync      -64.807857  0\n16,1024,15,4096,sync,0.3,0,0,4096,15  -16.913984  +1\n16,1024,5,4096,0,0.3,0,0,4096,15      -0.885328   +2\n\n\nasync #2 (current) vs. sync #2 (current - 2)\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n16,1024,15,0,sync,0.3,0,0,0,sync  -64.891073  0\n16,1024,5,4096,0,2,0,0,0,sync     -2.998018   -2\n16,1024,5,4096,0,0.3,0,0,4096,15  +1.055062   +2\n1,1024,sync,0,5,2,0,0,4096,15     -16.960175  0\n\n\nasync #2 (current) vs. async #1 (current - 1)\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n16,1024,15,0,sync,0.3,0,0,0,sync  -61.959043  0\n16,1024,5,4096,sync,2,0,0,0,15    +15.920144  0\n16,1024,15,4096,sync,2,0,0,0,15   +16.867793  0\n16,1024,0,0,sync,0.3,0,0,4096,15  +17.050724  0\n16,1024,5,4096,0,0.3,0,0,4096,15  +0.992007   +2\n```\n\nHere negative numbers are generally good. (I should make the benchmark tell you that...)\n",
